James inquired about the terminology used for equations of motion that relate a system of state variables over time. I explained that these are typically called **state equations**, **equations of motion**, or a **state-transition system**. I provided the discrete-time form of a state vector $\mathbf{x}_t$ defined by the state-transition function $\mathbf{F}$ and highlighted its continuous-time counterpart as $\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}(t), \mathbf{u}(t), \boldsymbol{\theta}, t)$. Additionally, I mentioned that when multiple state variables interact, it results in a **coupled dynamical system**.
